Inspiration
  The idea of typing by silent mouthing was prompted by Aarush Yadav's announcement of Subvocal
  (https://x.com/AarushYadav47/status/2116198463847408066, 30 October 2026), which uses glasses
  with non-contact sensing. Silent Dictation is an independent, camera-only, software experiment and
  is not affiliated with or endorsed by Subvocal.

Included source code
  third_party/espnet/
    Derived from ESPnet (https://github.com/espnet/espnet), Apache-2.0, as packaged with the visual
    front-end by Pingchuan Ma / Imperial College London in Auto-AVSR
    (https://github.com/mpc001/auto_avsr, Apache-2.0) and redistributed in Chaplin by Amanvir Parhar
    (https://github.com/amanvirparhar/chaplin, MIT). Modified here: Python 3.12+ compatibility
    (distutils removed), Apple MPS device support in CTC prefix scoring, unused debugger imports removed.
  silentdict/mean_face_68.npy
    Reference mean face from Auto-AVSR (Apache-2.0), used to align the mouth crop.
  silentdict/roi.py
    Re-implementation of the Auto-AVSR / Chaplin mouth-cropping procedure for MediaPipe 1.x landmarks.
  The real-time push-to-talk + LLM correction design follows Chaplin (MIT).
  prompts/harvard.txt
    Harvard sentences, lists 1-7, from the IEEE Recommended Practice for Speech Quality Measurements
    (1969), in the public domain.

Models downloaded by setup.sh (not included in this repository)
  Auto-AVSR visual speech recognition model trained on LRS3 (LRS3_V_WER19.1) and its subword language
  model, by Pingchuan Ma et al., mirrored on Hugging Face by Amanvir Parhar. The authors release these
  weights for non-commercial research use; they are not covered by this project's licence, and personal
  models fine-tuned from them inherit their terms.
  SentencePiece unigram5000 model from Auto-AVSR (Apache-2.0).
  MediaPipe Face Landmarker by Google (Apache-2.0).
  Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 (4-bit MLX conversion by mlx-community), by the Qwen team (Apache-2.0).
